260-/// Storage a list or a record's contents live in, shared rather than owned.
261-///
262-/// This is the one place Redblue's value semantics are implemented, and the
263-/// reason a *copy* of a list is not a *traversal* of it. Every value a program
264-/// binds is copied when it is read — a variable read, an argument, a returned
265-/// value — and with an owned `Vec` each of those copies walked the whole list,
266-/// so a loop that indexes a list of n cost n per index and a loop that appended
267-/// to one cost n per append. Redblue has no way to observe the difference: an
268-/// append still produces a new list for every other binding to see, because
269-/// [`Arc::make_mut`] copies whenever the storage is shared and only writes
270-/// through when the value holds the last handle.
271-///
272-/// The rule that keeps the meaning of a program unchanged is therefore: **read
273-/// by cloning the handle, write through [`Arc::make_mut`] and never through
274-/// `&mut`**. A `&mut Vec` reaching an element of a shared list is the one way
275-/// to make a change a program cannot see through the name it made it with.
276-pub type Shared<T> = std::sync::Arc<T>;
277-
278-impl Value {
