859-/// hole the table could open was the one hole the matrix could not see. Its guard
860-/// (`checked > BUILTIN_NAMES.len()`) did not catch that either, because a table
861-/// that grants everything still leaves hundreds of pairs to refuse — the guard
862-/// asked whether there was anything left to check, not whether the right things
863-/// were being checked. The documents cannot be widened the same way: writing
864-/// `csv.uppercase` into a code block is itself checked by
865-/// `edge_a_member_the_documents_name_is_a_function_the_registry_has`.
866-///
867-/// The two directions now meet in one place: a pair answers only if the table
868-/// holds it, a pair the table holds is written down by each document, and a pair
869-/// no document writes down is refused. Widening any one of the three fails a
870-/// gate, so there is nothing left to widen.
871-///
872-/// The whole matrix runs in-process: it is a property of name resolution, and a
873-/// refusal that a subprocess has to be spawned to observe is a refusal a hundred
